    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d229">

                <pb facs="0519" n="491" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v14p1/pg491"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">948.53/7–954: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 229<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in
                        the Republic of China</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d229-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >confidential<lb/>priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-07-09T20:22:00-04:00">July 9, 1954—8:22
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>24. Department concerned at continued Chinese detention Soviet tanker <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Tuapse</hi> and considers immediate release advisable.
                    Department knows of no grounds recognized by international law on which tanker
                    could be confiscated. You should take up matter urgently with Foreign Minister
                    pointing out apparent lack of legal warrant for continued detention of vessel
                    and crew, and risk of Soviet reprisals which might have far reaching
                    repercussions for other Governments as well as Chinese. You should ask to be
                    informed of approximate date of intended release of vessel and those of crew who
                    do not wish to claim political asylum.</p>
                <p>Ambassador Rankin has seen this message and concurs. If in your judgment your
                    hand would be strengthened thereby, you may request Foreign Minister to deliver
                    substance of foregoing to President <pb facs="0520" n="492" xml:id="pg492"/>
                    Chiang, prefaced by statement that Rankin has learned that high levels this
                    Government concerned over possible consequences further detention vessel and
                    crew.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d229-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d229-f1-content">
                <p>A note on the source text states that the telegram was sent at the direction of
                    the Under Secretary; it was drafted by McConaughy.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
